<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bettino Ricasoli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1880">1880</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bettino Ricasoli was a Tuscan baron and statesman who led the provisional government of Tuscany in 1859–1860, after the grand duke fled, and steered its union with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. He succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a> as prime minister of the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1861–1862 and served again in 1866–1867. Known as the Iron Baron, he also set down an early formula for Chianti wine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bettino-ricasoli.jpg" alt="Bettino Ricasoli" width="320" height="535" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Duroni &amp; Murer, Opticiens Photographes de S. M. Le Roi D'Italie, Paris, 12, Rue Vivienne, Milan, 11, Corso Vitt. Emanuel. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bettino_Ricasoli,_c._1860-1870_(frame_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Bettino Ricasoli</b> the provisional government joined the United Provinces of Central Italy, and a plebiscite in March 1860 approved annexation to the Kingdom of Sardinia; Florence was capital of Italy from 1865 to 1871.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Luigi Carlo Farini governed Modena, Parma and Romagna, united as Emilia, while <b>Bettino Ricasoli</b> led Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-provinces-of-central-italy">United Provinces of Central Italy</a></li></ul></section>
